WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:05.480
Speaker 1: So I want to start today with one
of the two big areas where there is a particularly

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:08.160
high amount of disinformation spreading right
now.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:13.470
Now, earlier in the week, we talked about
one such area and that's covered in vaccines.

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:15.700
The I don't even know what to call it.

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:17.279
It's almost like they're calling it.

00:00:17.279 --> 00:00:18.849
The launch seems weird.

00:00:18.849 --> 00:00:20.150
It's almost like a product.

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:25.669
But the breakthrough of the Omicron variant
into the news, which started on the day after

00:00:25.669 --> 00:00:30.970
Thanksgiving last week and led to two very,
very shaky days in the stock market.

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:37.879
That's one topic that has lent itself to significant
disinformation, right wing media and Republicans

00:00:37.879 --> 00:00:43.929
going crazy with false claims about the variant
itself, false claims about COVID, false claims

00:00:43.929 --> 00:00:46.160
about vaccines, et cetera, et cetera.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:47.500
We covered that Monday.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:50.230
We covered it to some extent yesterday, Tuesday.

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:56.129
The other area that is being victimized by
disinformation from the right at this point

00:00:56.129 --> 00:00:59.420
in time is the economy.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:07.690
And by the economy that includes the supply
chain stuff, employment, inflation and a few

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:09.290
other areas.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:15.230
That's another area of significant disinformation
with such esteemed talking points from the

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:21.300
right, as inflation is never going to end
and it's going to destroy the economy.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:26.240
Nobody wants to work anymore because of unemployment,
and there's no workers and the job situation

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:28.770
is a disaster, et cetera, et cetera.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:31.340
You guys have heard a lot of these talking
points.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:37.250
Oh, and also there's no Chinese made tchotchkes
for Christmas on the shelves.

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:38.640
Thanks to Joe Biden.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:45.670
OK, so those are the kind of general topics
when we actually look at the economic data

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:49.560
that is generally accepted as signaling how
the economy is doing now.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.390
I'll get back to that because that's not a
trivial matter.

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:57.140
There are measures we generally accept to
tell us about the economy.

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:59.750
We could say these are the wrong measures,
but we're going to get back to that.

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:04.020
When you look at those measures, things actually
look OK.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:09.110
A few new as we are now into December, a few
new bullet points of data that were released

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:10.340
today.

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:18.810
Initial jobless claims, meaning new unemployment
claims, fell to their lowest level last month,

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:25.260
November since November of 1969 on what's
called the seasonally adjusted basis.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:28.140
OK, that's the week before Thanksgiving.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:34.440
Now, unadjusted numbers increased and this
is an important thing to understand.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:40.230
There were a hundred and 99000 new unemployment
claims during the week ending November 20th,

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:41.940
the week before Thanksgiving.

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:45.710
Now, those have been what's called seasonally
adjusted.

00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:53.130
This is an accepted method for getting what
we might call a truer number, which controls

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:58.740
for the layoff patterns that are expected
based on the time of year that it is.

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:07.400
There are industries including retail, agriculture
and others, which have seasonal patterns for

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:14.110
hiring and laying people off, and they don't
really represent a true hire or layoff in

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:15.110
some sense.

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:16.900
So there's this process called adjustment
now.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:22.600
If you don't adjust the numbers, claims actually
did go up a little bit by 18000 to almost

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:25.380
two hundred and fifty nine thousand that week
before Thanksgiving.

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:31.709
But the general picture is that these are
very low numbers for new unemployment claims.

00:03:31.709 --> 00:03:33.330
So that's one side of the equation.

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.320
It actually looks pretty good.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:39.850
The Labor Department actually expected many
more people to apply for unemployment benefits

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:40.940
for the first time in that week.

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:43.660
Before Thanksgiving, they anticipated 70000
more.

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:48.770
It was actually only 18000 more, and that's
a good thing by this metric.

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:55.560
OK, now there's a question as to whether,
you know, I said the lowest in 52 years.

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:59.330
We have changed a number of things over that
fifty two year period.

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:02.980
And so there's a legitimate question as to
are we really comparing apples to apples when

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:08.800
we compare 2021 to 1969 or some period in
between because there have been changes in

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:13.930
that interim period, for example, some states
have made it harder to get unemployment, particularly

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:17.129
since the Great Recession of 2008 2009.

00:04:17.129 --> 00:04:21.269
So that would reduce the number who even apply
because they know it's harder to actually

00:04:21.269 --> 00:04:22.269
get the benefits.

00:04:22.269 --> 00:04:23.269
That's that that's fair.

00:04:23.269 --> 00:04:24.610
That could be a factor.

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:28.990
On the other hand, since the start of the
pandemic, more people are aware that such

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:30.779
benefits could be made available to them.

00:04:30.779 --> 00:04:36.309
So that would be a factor that compared to
the 70s and 80s, for example, makes more people

00:04:36.309 --> 00:04:37.319
apply.

00:04:37.319 --> 00:04:41.789
Generally speaking, you take a very top line
look, new unemployment claims are very, very

00:04:41.789 --> 00:04:48.349
low on an unadjusted basis, on an adjusted
basis, dating back five decades.

00:04:48.349 --> 00:04:51.379
That really pieces of six decades all the
way back to 1969.

00:04:51.379 --> 00:04:53.180
So it's one thing.

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:54.639
What about job creation?

00:04:54.639 --> 00:04:59.430
On the other side of unemployment is job creation
while private payrolls went up.

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:02.370
By five hundred and 34000 in November.

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:09.969
That's quite a bit better than the Dow Jones
expectation of 500 and 6000 new jobs expected

00:05:09.969 --> 00:05:11.830
was 506.

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:14.249
Actual was 534.

00:05:14.249 --> 00:05:17.620
Leisure and hospitality soar again.

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:21.770
Companies with more than 500 employees saw
a big gain.

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:26.189
Companies with over a thousand employees saw
an even bigger proportional gain.

00:05:26.189 --> 00:05:30.029
And so that's all pretty good by the standards
that we use.

00:05:30.029 --> 00:05:34.460
So the question marks now are what happens
with Omicron and to what degree does that

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:37.059
impact the economy, at least in the U.S.?

00:05:37.059 --> 00:05:40.529
And this is an economic statement I'm making,
not a public health statement.

00:05:40.529 --> 00:05:45.990
There does not seem to be much, if any, appetite
for anything that would approximate a shut

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:48.659
down or a lockdown of any kind.

00:05:48.659 --> 00:05:52.580
Now, whether it will make sense from a public
health standpoint, first, we need to know

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:54.009
how serious Omicron is.

00:05:54.009 --> 00:05:55.650
We still don't.

00:05:55.650 --> 00:06:00.219
But that's a public health question dealing
with this as an economic question.

00:06:00.219 --> 00:06:03.220
It doesn't seem like there's really much appetite
for that.

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:06.189
What happens with supply chain?

00:06:06.189 --> 00:06:08.110
And are these even good metrics?

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:10.319
And that's something that is a different question.

00:06:10.319 --> 00:06:14.629
You know, there are systemic problems with
the economy that I talk about all the time

00:06:14.629 --> 00:06:18.129
that I know many of you are aware of, which
aren't part of this analysis.

00:06:18.129 --> 00:06:23.999
When we look at new unemployment claims or
job creation or whatever, we are talking about

00:06:23.999 --> 00:06:29.080
wage growth, we're not talking about the reality
that the minimum wage in most of the United

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:33.080
States is not a living wage as defined by.

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:37.659
Can you even get a basic two bedroom apartment
in the place where you are employed at the

00:06:37.659 --> 00:06:38.710
minimum wage?

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:42.969
Those are systemic problems that still exist,
and there's no question that the metrics we

00:06:42.969 --> 00:06:45.680
use fall woefully short.

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:51.930
That being said, the right accepts these metrics
and so on their own terms.

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:56.399
It's very difficult to argue that we have
a bad economy right now.

00:06:56.399 --> 00:06:57.639
We haven't solved.

00:06:57.639 --> 00:07:00.029
Not everybody has health care.

00:07:00.029 --> 00:07:01.169
That doesn't change.

00:07:01.169 --> 00:07:05.110
But by the standards the right accepts, things
are actually looking OK.

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:09.479
And it's important to to understand that when
you hear they're despairing talking points

00:07:09.479 --> 00:08:04.619
on Fox News 

00:08:04.619 --> 00:08:07.069
and elsewhere.

